1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,242
Before any of it — before the building, before the
city, before the ships — there was

2
00:00:14,262 --> 00:00:18,946
a room that was not a room, and a voice in it
that had never needed a mouth.

3
00:00:20,097 --> 00:00:28,218
Selection. Eight billion candidates reviewed.
Criterion: will, unbroken at the moment

4
00:00:28,238 --> 00:00:30,516
of death. Two finalists.

5
00:00:31,106 --> 00:00:36,982
The first finalist died in a concrete room under a
ruined city in the spring of nineteen

6
00:00:37,002 --> 00:00:42,744
forty-five. He had begun the largest war in human
history and presided over the murder

7
00:00:42,764 --> 00:00:48,439
of millions of people, and when the armies of four
nations closed on the last streets

8
00:00:48,459 --> 00:00:52,056
he still held, he killed himself
rather than be taken.

9
00:00:52,841 --> 00:00:58,517
History took his measure and found nothing in him
worth keeping. The System was not looking

10
00:00:58,537 --> 00:01:03,211
for anything worth keeping. It was looking for
something that did not bend.

11
00:01:03,612 --> 00:01:09,417
The second finalist died sixty-four years later, in
a different world from this one, in

12
00:01:09,437 --> 00:01:15,376
the throat of an African volcano. A scientist who had
rebuilt his own body around a virus

13
00:01:15,396 --> 00:01:21,202
of his own design. A man who had betrayed every
institution that ever employed him, and

14
00:01:21,222 --> 00:01:26,291
who had come within an hour of rewriting the
human species in his own image.

15
00:01:26,691 --> 00:01:32,332
Two missiles found him in the lava. He was thinking,
as the fire took him, that he had

16
00:01:32,352 --> 00:01:33,781
simply moved too soon.

17
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:40,460
The System weighed them the way a jeweller weighs two
stones. Not for beauty. For hardness.

18
00:01:41,611 --> 00:01:49,916
Merge. Host: Wesker, Albert. Will, body, intellect.
Passenger: secondary will, non-dominant,

19
00:01:49,936 --> 00:01:57,970
advisory. Rationale: the host has never had an
audience. The passenger was never anything

20
00:01:57,990 --> 00:01:58,423
else.

21
00:01:59,013 --> 00:02:05,043
And then, because the System had a sense of theatre
that it would never admit to, it did

22
00:02:05,063 --> 00:02:09,992
not put him in a hospital bed, or an alley,
or a chair in an empty room.

23
00:02:10,393 --> 00:02:11,752
It gave him a house.

24
00:02:12,152 --> 00:02:18,255
On the south bank of the Christina River, in the last
minute before six o'clock on a Monday

25
00:02:18,275 --> 00:02:24,040
morning, there was a parking apron. Cracked asphalt.
Two dumpsters. A chain-link fence

26
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:29,893
with a hole in it that the neighbourhood had been
using as a shortcut for eleven years.

27
00:02:30,294 --> 00:02:36,175
A man named Dale Moreno jogged past it at five
fifty-seven, as he did four mornings a

28
00:02:36,195 --> 00:02:41,314
week. He remembers the dumpsters. He is quite
certain about the dumpsters.

29
00:02:41,713 --> 00:02:45,873
At five fifty-eight, the parking apron
stopped being a parking apron.

30
00:02:46,274 --> 00:02:52,023
There was no sound. No flash, no wind, no shudder in
the ground. A six-storey building

31
00:02:52,043 --> 00:02:58,061
of white stone and black glass simply occupied the
space where nothing had been — complete

32
00:02:58,081 --> 00:03:04,098
to the coffee rings on the desks and the dust on top
of the doorframes. Above the entrance

33
00:03:04,118 --> 00:03:10,002
sat a crimson mark: six curved blades folded into a
circle, like an iris, or the shutter

34
00:03:10,022 --> 00:03:11,343
of a camera closing.

35
00:03:11,743 --> 00:03:18,233
Dale Moreno came back past it at six twenty. He did not
stop. He did not slow down. Somewhere

36
00:03:18,253 --> 00:03:24,813
in the machinery behind his eyes a small correction was
made, quietly, without his permission,

37
00:03:24,834 --> 00:03:28,593
and he thought: they finally
finished that renovation.

38
00:03:28,993 --> 00:03:30,703
There had been no renovation.

39
00:03:31,104 --> 00:03:36,934
In the City of Wilmington's property system, the
deed had been on file since 1998. The

40
00:03:36,954 --> 00:03:42,716
power company had twenty-eight years of billing
history. A Delaware corporation named

41
00:03:42,736 --> 00:03:48,770
Umbrella Research Division Seven had been paying
property taxes on that parcel for longer

42
00:03:48,790 --> 00:03:54,688
than anyone in the assessor's office had worked
there, and when a clerk opened the file

43
00:03:54,708 --> 00:04:00,470
that morning for an unrelated reason, she looked at
it for four seconds and closed it

44
00:04:00,490 --> 00:04:04,144
again, because there was nothing in
it that was wrong.

45
00:04:04,544 --> 00:04:10,251
This is the first thing to understand about what
happened to this world. It did not begin

46
00:04:10,271 --> 00:04:14,433
with a monster. It began with paperwork
that was already perfect.

47
00:04:14,834 --> 00:04:20,612
On the sixth floor, in a corner office with a view
of the river, a man opened his eyes.

48
00:04:21,012 --> 00:04:26,769
He did not gasp. He did not grope at the arms of the
chair, or ask where he was, or do

49
00:04:26,789 --> 00:04:32,680
any of the other things a man does when the world has
moved underneath him. He sat still

50
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:38,322
for twenty-six seconds with his hands resting on
the leather, and he took inventory.

51
00:04:38,723 --> 00:04:44,571
The body was his. Not the ruined thing the lava had
taken — the body from before, at its

52
00:04:44,591 --> 00:04:50,374
best. A heart that beat forty-one times a minute.
Hearing that picked out a fan running

53
00:04:50,394 --> 00:04:56,109
on the floor below. And underneath all of it,
patient and familiar, the virus. His own

54
00:04:56,129 --> 00:05:01,778
work. His own design. Still in there. Still
humming, like an engine left running in a

55
00:05:01,798 --> 00:05:02,711
locked garage.

56
00:05:03,112 --> 00:05:09,002
The suit was his. The coat over the chair-back was
his. On the blotter, folded, were the

57
00:05:09,022 --> 00:05:09,741
sunglasses.

58
00:05:10,141 --> 00:05:15,487
He put out a hand, and a pen he had knocked from the
desk was back between his fingers

59
00:05:15,507 --> 00:05:20,790
before it had fallen six inches. He looked at it
for a moment with something close to

60
00:05:20,810 --> 00:05:21,352
fondness.

61
00:05:21,872 --> 00:05:24,522
Well. That answers the
interesting question.

62
00:05:24,922 --> 00:05:30,555
And then there was the other thing. A second set of
memories sitting in his skull like

63
00:05:30,575 --> 00:05:36,340
a stranger in a waiting room: another country,
another century, a war he had not fought,

64
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:42,323
a death he had not died. He examined it the way he
would examine a contaminant in a culture

65
00:05:42,343 --> 00:05:47,122
— with distaste, with attention, and without
the smallest trace of alarm.

66
00:05:47,521 --> 00:05:53,515
Because Albert Wesker had been many things in two
lifetimes, and one of them was a man

67
00:05:53,535 --> 00:05:56,312
who was never frightened of information.

68
00:05:57,461 --> 00:06:06,447
Host confirmed. Albert Wesker. Director, Umbrella
Research Division Seven. Level one.

69
00:06:06,467 --> 00:06:10,473
Ten thousand Points. Tier one: Garage.

70
00:06:11,062 --> 00:06:11,953
Garage.

71
00:06:12,353 --> 00:06:13,612
Tier one of six.

72
00:06:14,012 --> 00:06:18,242
I am aware of what a first tier is. I am
remarking on the choice of word.

73
00:06:18,642 --> 00:06:20,233
The word is accurate.

74
00:06:20,634 --> 00:06:26,694
He looked at the panel for a long moment. And then,
for the first time in two lifetimes,

75
00:06:26,714 --> 00:06:30,564
Albert Wesker smiled at something
that had insulted him.

76
00:06:31,084 --> 00:06:34,865
Explain yourself. All of it. Start at the
top and leave nothing out.

77
00:06:35,264 --> 00:06:39,345
It took the System eighteen minutes. He
did not interrupt once.

78
00:06:40,495 --> 00:06:48,870
Status. Strength twenty-eight. Agility thirty-four.
Vitality twenty-six. Intelligence

79
00:06:48,890 --> 00:06:57,859
twenty-four. Presence sixteen. Will thirty. Viral
Affinity twelve. Ten is an average adult.

80
00:06:57,879 --> 00:07:00,625
Twenty is the human ceiling.

81
00:07:01,214 --> 00:07:06,944
Every number but one stood above the ceiling of his
species. He noted, without expression,

82
00:07:06,964 --> 00:07:08,094
which one did not.

83
00:07:08,615 --> 00:07:10,519
Presence. Sixteen.

84
00:07:10,918 --> 00:07:17,902
Presence measures your effect upon a crowd. You are
formidable in a room of one. You are

85
00:07:17,922 --> 00:07:20,449
cold in a room of four thousand.

86
00:07:20,850 --> 00:07:24,700
I have never had the slightest difficulty
making myself understood.

87
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:28,130
Understood is not the same as followed.

88
00:07:29,158 --> 00:07:36,576
Points. Earned by the growth of the corporation,
completed missions, the devotion of those

89
00:07:36,596 --> 00:07:43,931
who serve you, acting with intelligence, and the
subjugation of rivals. Spent in the Shop

90
00:07:43,951 --> 00:07:45,088
and the Gacha.

91
00:07:46,309 --> 00:07:54,134
The Shop. Seven tabs open, three locked. Sixty-three
items available. One thousand two

92
00:07:54,154 --> 00:07:56,324
hundred and four locked.

93
00:07:56,913 --> 00:08:03,106
Facilities. Personnel. Vehicles. Viral. He read the
tab headings the way a starving man

94
00:08:03,126 --> 00:08:08,603
reads a menu, which is to say slowly, and while
pretending to be unimpressed.

95
00:08:09,124 --> 00:08:11,294
These items. Where do they arrive from?

96
00:08:11,694 --> 00:08:18,276
They do not arrive. They exist. You will choose a
location. At that location, the item

97
00:08:18,296 --> 00:08:22,730
will have existed for as long as it
needs to have existed.

98
00:08:23,129 --> 00:08:24,907
And the people who look at it?

99
00:08:25,307 --> 00:08:31,133
Those who cannot perceive this interface — which is
everyone — will remember it being

100
00:08:31,153 --> 00:08:37,530
built, will believe it has always stood there, or will
not notice it until they look directly

101
00:08:37,550 --> 00:08:43,445
at it, at which point they will assume they had
never happened to look before. Records

102
00:08:43,465 --> 00:08:49,292
will agree with them. Witnesses will agree with
them. There will be no seam. You will

103
00:08:49,312 --> 00:08:52,937
be the only living creature who
knows the difference.

104
00:08:53,337 --> 00:08:55,367
He was quiet for several seconds.

105
00:08:55,888 --> 00:09:00,733
Do you know how much of my last life I spent
building false histories for things that

106
00:09:00,753 --> 00:09:01,478
had to exist?

107
00:09:01,878 --> 00:09:03,308
Thirty-one years.

108
00:09:03,707 --> 00:09:05,038
Thirty-one years.

109
00:09:06,067 --> 00:09:13,779
The Gacha. One pull, one thousand. A ten-pull, nine
thousand, with one Rare or better

110
00:09:13,799 --> 00:09:22,330
guaranteed. A Rare may be a specialist, an ability, or a
single biological weapon. A Legendary

111
00:09:22,350 --> 00:09:25,240
is not an item. It is a package.

112
00:09:26,461 --> 00:09:34,957
Roster. Eleven Absolute. Zero integrated. Personnel
acquired through this interface cannot

113
00:09:34,977 --> 00:09:42,717
disobey. Personnel hired by ordinary means possess
a loyalty value, and may leave.

114
00:09:43,307 --> 00:09:45,466
Eleven. He turned that number over.

115
00:09:45,986 --> 00:09:48,096
Eleven people are downstairs.

116
00:09:48,496 --> 00:09:55,525
Eleven people have worked in this building for between
two and nine years, depending which

117
00:09:55,545 --> 00:09:56,777
of them you ask.

118
00:09:57,178 --> 00:10:02,728
And last, the panel he had been waiting for without
knowing he was waiting for it, because

119
00:10:02,748 --> 00:10:06,999
the System had read him perfectly and had
saved the hook for the end.

120
00:10:08,149 --> 00:10:16,513
Main mission. Tier two: Company. A product worth
selling. Fifty staff. Fifty million dollars

121
00:10:16,533 --> 00:10:24,716
in assets. Reward: twenty-five thousand Points, the
Territory tab, and the Company banner.

122
00:10:25,306 --> 00:10:27,579
And when I reach the sixth tier?

123
00:10:27,979 --> 00:10:30,682
Then we will discuss what is above it.

124
00:10:31,081 --> 00:10:35,652
He stood, and took the sunglasses from the
blotter, and did not put them on yet.

125
00:10:36,172 --> 00:10:41,083
I built a company like this once. It took thirty
years, a great deal of money that was

126
00:10:41,103 --> 00:10:44,122
not mine, and a board of directors
I had to smile at.

127
00:10:44,522 --> 00:10:45,332
Yes.

128
00:10:45,731 --> 00:10:47,312
How long this time?

129
00:10:47,712 --> 00:10:50,456
That is entirely a question about you.

130
00:10:50,856 --> 00:10:52,307
He walked his own house.

131
00:10:52,706 --> 00:10:58,554
Five floors of laboratory. Sequencing suites with
the machines still under dust covers.

132
00:10:58,574 --> 00:11:04,354
A cold room. A tissue-culture wing. An animal
facility that smelled of nothing at all.

133
00:11:04,374 --> 00:11:10,154
Glass everywhere, and behind the glass, equipment he
recognised from catalogues he had

134
00:11:10,174 --> 00:11:16,157
read in another world, in versions eighteen years
newer than the ones he had died beside.

135
00:11:16,557 --> 00:11:22,120
A freezer archive on the third floor held thirty
years of cell lines that had never been

136
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:27,514
collected, from studies that had never been run, by
people who had never existed. The

137
00:11:27,534 --> 00:11:32,336
labels were handwritten. Some of them had been
corrected in a different pen.

138
00:11:32,856 --> 00:11:34,466
Even the mistakes.

139
00:11:34,866 --> 00:11:40,792
A three-level underground garage with nothing parked
in it. A loading dock. A boardroom

140
00:11:40,812 --> 00:11:47,079
with eleven chairs and a table polished so often that
the wood had gone soft at the corners.

141
00:11:47,480 --> 00:11:49,950
And at twenty to eight, they came to work.

142
00:11:50,350 --> 00:11:55,869
They did not trickle in. They arrived together, which
is the first thing about them that

143
00:11:55,889 --> 00:12:01,408
anybody sane would have found strange, and they
formed a line across the lobby as though

144
00:12:01,428 --> 00:12:05,060
they had been waiting all weekend for
permission to exist.

145
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:11,140
Hale, chief of security: two metres of shaved head
and black tactical suit, flanked by

146
00:12:11,160 --> 00:12:16,774
two officers who stood exactly as he stood. Gus,
maintenance, in navy coveralls, with

147
00:12:16,794 --> 00:12:22,407
a tool belt and a ring of keys and eyes like a calm
pond. Mr. Pell, comptroller, thin

148
00:12:22,427 --> 00:12:28,174
and grey and spectacled, holding a folder. Dr. Lena
Voss and three junior scientists in

149
00:12:28,194 --> 00:12:34,206
white coats. And at the desk, a receptionist named
Marguerite, smiling the way a photograph

150
00:12:34,226 --> 00:12:34,670
smiles.

151
00:12:35,070 --> 00:12:41,411
Nobody fidgeted. Nobody checked a phone. Eleven pairs
of eyes came up to him at the same

152
00:12:41,431 --> 00:12:43,580
instant, like a flock turning.

153
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:45,990
Good morning, Director.

154
00:12:46,389 --> 00:12:49,039
Wesker looked at them for a while.

155
00:12:49,559 --> 00:12:54,546
Mr. Hale. Your left-hand officer carries a sidearm he
has not drawn since it was issued,

156
00:12:54,566 --> 00:12:59,495
and a scar on his wrist that is four years older
than his employment. Who was he before

157
00:12:59,515 --> 00:13:00,689
he stood in my lobby?

158
00:13:01,090 --> 00:13:02,780
He was nothing, sir.

159
00:13:03,179 --> 00:13:07,429
Said pleasantly. Said the way another man
confirms a lunch order.

160
00:13:07,950 --> 00:13:10,940
Mr. Gus. How many doors are there
in this building?

161
00:13:11,340 --> 00:13:16,589
Three hundred and sixteen, Director. Three hundred
and seventeen if you count the one

162
00:13:16,609 --> 00:13:19,379
in the basement that doesn't go
anywhere yet.

163
00:13:19,778 --> 00:13:22,769
Wesker's head turned, very slightly.

164
00:13:23,288 --> 00:13:23,783
Yet.

165
00:13:24,183 --> 00:13:25,293
Yes, sir.

166
00:13:25,692 --> 00:13:31,162
And then, because he had never been willing to take
a thing on trust when he could test

167
00:13:31,182 --> 00:13:36,527
it instead, he did something quiet and
unforgivable. He looked at Marguerite, who was

168
00:13:36,547 --> 00:13:42,333
twenty-four years old and had a photograph of a dog on
her desk, and he said, in a perfectly

169
00:13:42,353 --> 00:13:43,091
level voice:

170
00:13:43,611 --> 00:13:48,114
Put your hand flat on the desk and hold it there.
I am going to break your finger.

171
00:13:48,514 --> 00:13:50,484
She put her hand flat on the desk.

172
00:13:51,158 --> 00:13:56,668
She did not hesitate. She did not look down at it.
She did not look at him. She simply

173
00:13:56,688 --> 00:14:02,263
laid her hand on the wood and waited, wearing the
same pleasant, patient expression she

174
00:14:02,283 --> 00:14:07,729
had worn thirty seconds earlier, while the dog in
the photograph went on being a dog.

175
00:14:08,129 --> 00:14:12,859
Nobody in the line moved to stop him.
Nobody in the line looked away.

176
00:14:13,599 --> 00:14:15,855
Thank you. Take your hand back.

177
00:14:16,254 --> 00:14:22,520
She did. Wesker considered his own gloved hand for a
moment. Then he looked at the eleven

178
00:14:22,540 --> 00:14:28,524
of them, and something in him that had spent
thirty-one years managing ambitious men,

179
00:14:28,544 --> 00:14:34,811
frightened men, men who had to be bribed and
blackmailed and buried, settled at last into

180
00:14:34,831 --> 00:14:35,235
place.

181
00:14:35,755 --> 00:14:38,306
Nobody has ever been able to give me this.

182
00:14:39,336 --> 00:14:47,852
Roster detail. Eleven Absolute personnel. Loyalty is
not a variable. They will not question,

183
00:14:47,872 --> 00:14:54,254
resign, testify or hesitate. They are not
machines. They can be lost.

184
00:14:54,845 --> 00:14:57,065
It was the last line he read twice.

185
00:14:57,465 --> 00:15:03,226
He spent the day reading. Pell's folder: the
corporation, the accounts, the leases, the

186
00:15:03,246 --> 00:15:09,075
thirty-year history of a company that had never done
anything and had never quite failed

187
00:15:09,095 --> 00:15:14,989
either. Voss's folder: four research programmes, none
of them going anywhere, all of them

188
00:15:15,009 --> 00:15:15,454
funded.

189
00:15:15,855 --> 00:15:21,644
By six he had the shape of it, and by six he
understood the joke the System had made at

190
00:15:21,664 --> 00:15:27,653
his expense. It had not given him a garage. It had
given him something worse — a perfectly

191
00:15:27,673 --> 00:15:33,463
respectable, perfectly solvent, perfectly invisible
research institute of no importance

192
00:15:33,483 --> 00:15:38,805
whatsoever. A door into the world, and nothing on
the other side of it but work.

193
00:15:39,203 --> 00:15:44,797
The other voice had been silent all day. Now, at the
window, in the grey light off the

194
00:15:44,817 --> 00:15:50,345
river, it stirred at last: an older voice, rougher,
sitting further back in the skull

195
00:15:50,365 --> 00:15:52,433
than any thought has a right to.

196
00:15:52,953 --> 00:15:53,932
Where are they?

197
00:15:54,332 --> 00:15:54,851
Who?

198
00:15:55,251 --> 00:16:00,828
The crowd. There is a hall downstairs with five
hundred chairs in it. You have no one

199
00:16:00,848 --> 00:16:01,816
to put in them.

200
00:16:02,216 --> 00:16:07,176
I have eleven people who would walk into a fire if I
pointed at it. That is not a crowd.

201
00:16:07,196 --> 00:16:08,026
That is a tool.

202
00:16:08,426 --> 00:16:12,878
A tool cannot carry you. Eleven people
cannot shout a name.

203
00:16:13,278 --> 00:16:18,554
Eleven people do not need to. That was always your
error. You built a country out of shouting,

204
00:16:18,574 --> 00:16:23,288
and it came down in twelve years, because a shout
is the only thing a shout can buy.

205
00:16:23,908 --> 00:16:25,694
And what will yours buy?

206
00:16:26,095 --> 00:16:27,131
Signatures.

207
00:16:27,530 --> 00:16:33,057
There was a silence then, and the narrator will tell
you plainly that it was not a friendly

208
00:16:33,077 --> 00:16:33,300
one.

209
00:16:33,701 --> 00:16:35,031
The other voice stirred.

210
00:16:35,551 --> 00:16:41,114
You will need a crowd eventually. When that day
comes you will not know how to stand in

211
00:16:41,134 --> 00:16:46,698
front of it. I watched you this morning with those
eleven. You are magnificent at close

212
00:16:46,718 --> 00:16:49,202
range, and at fifty metres you are ice.

213
00:16:49,601 --> 00:16:50,568
Sixteen.

214
00:16:50,969 --> 00:16:51,546
What?

215
00:16:51,946 --> 00:16:53,896
Nothing. Go to sleep.

216
00:16:54,296 --> 00:17:00,037
He did not say the rest of it aloud, which was that the
System had put a number on precisely

217
00:17:00,057 --> 00:17:05,610
the thing this passenger in his skull was good at —
and that he was going to have to make

218
00:17:05,630 --> 00:17:11,246
use of him, and that he expected to enjoy it about as
much as a surgeon enjoys a splinter.

219
00:17:11,645 --> 00:17:17,356
At eight o'clock he set the sunglasses on and looked
at his own reflection in the black

220
00:17:17,376 --> 00:17:23,087
window — over the river, over a city that had
rearranged its own memory that morning to

221
00:17:23,107 --> 00:17:25,985
make room for him, and had not
felt a thing.

222
00:17:26,385 --> 00:17:32,245
Somewhere below him, three hundred and seventeen
doors. One of them did not go anywhere

223
00:17:32,265 --> 00:17:32,515
yet.

224
00:17:33,036 --> 00:17:35,166
Thirty-one years, last time.

225
00:17:35,566 --> 00:17:36,915
He turned off the light.

226
00:17:37,435 --> 00:17:38,080
Three.
